From d464573450527fcdf5cb9ff27f005e60672a2c21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ren=C3=A9=20Rebe?= <rene@exactco.de>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 13:50:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] powerpc: Add NuBus Power Macintosh support
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add the first Power Macintosh generation with NuBus slots, based on the
nubus-pmac port (Takashi Oe, Tobias Netzel), MkLinux osfmk and the m68k
Macintosh code: the PDM family (6100, 7100, 8100, Workgroup Server; needs a
601 kernel), the Performa/Power Macintosh 5200/6200 and the M2 PowerBooks
(5300, 1400, Duo 2300).  Device trees are built in and selected by the Gestalt
machine ID, as BootX passes none on these machines.

Platform:
 - PDM AMIC interrupt controller, in level mode as in MkLinux, using its latched
   mode only while a disabled source without a hardware mask is asserted
 - Performa Capella and M2 interrupt controller with the Baboon cascade, all
   pending sources dispatched at once as the CPU interrupt is latched
 - Cuda (ADB, RTC, restart, power off) and M2 PMU, ADB probing on this platform
 - 603 timebase measured with VIA timer 1, shared 256 KB AMIC DMA buffer area
 - NuBus bus core with arch hooks for the slot space and bus presence, machine
   check recovered hwreg_present(), slot interrupts through the device tree;
   mac8390 enabled, m68k behaviour unchanged

Drivers:
 - serial: pmac_zilog through its platform path, selected at run time
 - SCSI: amic_esp (PDM 53C94/53CF94, AMIC DMA through a bounce buffer),
   performa_esp (53C96, PIO by default), mac_scsi on the M2
 - IDE: pata_of_platform for the F108 and Baboon ports
 - Ethernet: amic_mace (MACE with AMIC DMA)
 - graphics: pdmfb (PDM on-board with mode setting and CLUT; boot mode and
   CLUT on the HPV and AV cards), valkyriefb on the Performa
 - sound: amic_awacs (PDM), primetime_asc (Performa, playback only)
 - floppy: swim3 with AMIC DMA, the macio code behind accessors and DMA ops

Boot:
 - BootX entry on NuBus, also without PPC_PMAC (PPC_BOOTX): built-in device
   tree, memory, boot display and initrd from the boot infos, which move to a
   memory bank that holds them
 - book3s/32 kernel above physical 0 with the linear map at 0, the default for
   these machines at 2 MB: the PDM on-board video scans RAM at 0x100000, which
   is reserved no-map; the exception vectors are redirected in head
 - nubus_pmac_defconfig (601, 6.4 MiB including bss, for the 8 MB bank 0),
   nubus_pmac_603_defconfig, nubus_pmac_8mb_defconfig

Generic fixes included:
 - prom_init: reset the length modifier for each conversion
 - BootX: sizes instead of end addresses in the memory reserve map, and an
   8 byte aligned flattened device tree
 - prom: align the memory size to 16 MB only for book3s/64
   (fixes 5ca096161cdc "powerpc/mm: Align memory_limit value specified using
   mem= kernel parameter")
 - esp_scsi: set the DMA length of identity mapped sg entries
 - pmac_zilog: unregister the console before disposing the ports
 - book3s/32: 64 KB early hash table without KASAN, physical address
   conversions by PAGE_OFFSET
 - boot wrapper: decompress at the kernel's physical start, refuse a
   compression wrapper.a cannot decompress

Tested in MAME pmac8100/pmac6100 (601) and pmac6200 (603) with an ELF quickload
and a BootX hand-over stub, with a high level Cuda model; the M2 drivers on a
test machine modelled from the same sources as the drivers, which validates the
software paths only.  QEMU 40p, g3beige regressions.  Nothing has run on real
hardware yet.  To check there:
 - AMIC latched mode, floppy DMA and the blank bit (MAME only); Performa ATA
   interrupt routing (MAME and MkLinux differ), EASC version and rate
 - machine check on empty NuBus slots, which also taints the kernel
 - M2: interrupt register latch and encoding, VIA2 and Baboon flag behaviour,
   PMU protocol and firmware, IDE byte order, 5380 pseudo DMA offsets
 - where a real BootX places the kernel and boot infos on these machines

Signed-off-by: René Rebe <rene@exactco.de>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
